 Within neuromechanical simulation, the controller built here belongs to the biologically grounded SNS line, one of the three lines compared in Section~\ref{sec:bg_neuromech}; the reflex-based and learned alternatives \citep{geyer_musclereflex_2010, song_deep_2021} are treated there. The ancestry is direct: the two-level organization implemented throughout this chapter is the rat-hindlimb CPG of \citet{deng_neuromechanical_2019}, built on the full-muscle hindlimb analysis of \citet{young_analyzing_2019}, and the research community and tooling around the approach, including SNS-Toolbox \citep{nourse_sns_2023}, are surveyed in Section~\ref{sec:bg_sns}. Two-level CPG control has reached musculoskeletal macaques and muscle-driven robotic hind legs \citep{nishizaki_gait_2026, fukuoka_autonomous_2021}, though on non-humanoid platforms or with generic musculature. What this program adds is the combination the background identifies as unclaimed: an anatomically grounded two-level controller with Ia, Ib, group II, and contact feedback, driving BPA-actuated models characterized in Chapters~\ref{ch:methods}--\ref{ch:torque}.
 
-This chapter concerns tooling: its object is a verified neuromechanical laboratory for the BPA-actuated robot. In that laboratory a controller can be built in any of several simulation engines, checked against its own re-implementations in the other engines and against the published behavior of the models it descends from, and then interrogated with the stimulus and deletion experiments that the locomotion literature applies to the biological circuits themselves. None of the infrastructure components is invented here: OpenSim, MuJoCo, MyoConverter, SNS-Toolbox, AnimatLab, Simulink and Simscape Multibody, SCONE, and the Sensory Afferent Database (SADb) of Chapter~\ref{ch:database} all existed before this program, and each is used for what it does best. What is new is the combination: the full-lower-body pipeline that converts the robot's OpenSim model to MuJoCo and couples it to an SNS-Toolbox spinal network; a derivative SNS block library for Simulink and Simscape Multibody; the Deng-lineage and Li-lineage controllers re-implemented across those programs and onto the converted models; and the SolidWorks robot assembly brought into Simscape Multibody. The distinction matters. Re-running an existing model in the environment it was built in, as the prior walker was run in AnimatLab, reproduces the original publication and adds nothing; re-implementing the same controller in a second and third environment, and checking it against the first, is what makes the circuits portable objects rather than artifacts of one simulator, and the engine differences encountered on the way are part of the result rather than a nuisance. The demonstrations are therefore kept at the scale of a knee reflex or a replicated gait, because a tool that works at that scale does not need a walking demonstration to justify itself; the heavier evaluation series that do appear are reported for the questions they answer.
