 The chapter's contribution is the tool set. The OpenSim--MyoConverter--SNS-Toolbox pipeline couples the full-lower-body converted model to a two-level spinal network with Ia, Ib, group II, and contact feedback, and it runs the full neuromechanical loop faster than real time. The SNS-Simscape library assembles the same component models in a standard controls environment, with the reflex demonstrations, the diameter-specific BPA force blocks, the exported production network, and the imported OpenSim and SolidWorks bodies behind it. The controller ports put the Deng-lineage and Li-lineage circuits into NumPy, Simulink, MuJoCo, and Simscape Multibody realizations on the same bodies, and the verification instruments (the cross-engine harness, the structure audits, the perturbation and basin gates, and the spiking mirrors) are what make the ports trustworthy. Every one of these tools is reusable by a researcher who did not build them, and the loop's speed is what makes deletion and stimulus experiments cheap enough to repeat.
 
-The laboratory's limits are on record with equal weight. The archived walkers do not walk unsupported on the ground; the tuned networks transfer poorly to diverse human reference cycles and walk right-leg-only or asymmetrically under the harness; the systematic stimulation series at the pattern-formation and motoneuron levels, and the motoneuron-level deletion phenomena of the reference models, are not yet reproduced; the SolidWorks knee arrives in Simscape Multibody with its joint welded pending re-mating; and the Shinohara, Di Russo, and Shevtsova replications remain circuit-rule translations rather than running models. What the tools are for is the program they define next, in Chapter~\ref{ch:futurework}: the balance and weight-bearing layers that unsupported walking requires, the systematic stimulation series at the motoneuron, pattern-formation, and rhythm-generator levels against which the circuits' signs, gains, and phase structure will be validated, and the transfer of the whole stack toward real-time hardware.
